{"statement": "The safetyist stance correctly identifies that complex models fail unpredictably when pushed outside their narrow training distributions, yet treating this reality as a justification for federal pre-deployment gating mechanisms misdiagnoses the engineering constraint. Training cycles for frontier models require months of compute cluster allocation across thousands of specialized accelerators, making rapid post-incident patching an economic impossibility rather than a solvable bureaucratic hurdle. When a 70-billion-parameter architecture drifts in production, developers cannot simply execute a hotfix; they face a multi-million-dollar retraining loop that renders static pre-market audits obsolete before the ink dries on the compliance filing.

Mandatory federal certification frameworks do not protect the public from algorithmic harm; they construct an insurmountable financial moat for dominant technology firms with the capital to absorb endless compliance overhead. A startup attempting to deploy open-weight weights cannot afford the legal and validation teams required by aviation-style lifecycle oversight, leaving the market entirely to three or four vertically integrated monopolies. Federal agencies lack the compute clusters and real-time telemetry access required to audit these models, meaning that statutory compliance regimes function merely as liability shields for big tech while small innovators absorb the regulatory penalty.

